@startuml product-variability-objects

' Two products, each showing a different legal shape, rather than one
' product forced to carry every case (harder to read, not more informative).
' "Classic Tee" is the same worked example as
' reference/schema/product-catalogue-worked-example.md, extended here with
' cases that example doesn't cover. "Plain Mug" is new — the simplest legal
' shape the schema allows: one variation, zero images anywhere.
'
' Together they show what a class diagram of this schema cannot:
'  - TWO pivots sharing one vocabulary table (attribute_values used for
'    both axis and description) — see "Two pivots over one vocabulary" in
'    explanation/product-variability.md
'  - price and image are COMPUTED at read time, never stored twice
'  - the image pivot's cardinality is genuinely many-to-many: one variation
'    can show several images, one image can appear on several variations,
'    and two variations' galleries can overlap without being identical
'  - ProductSpecification is a THIRD, unrelated table — free-text
'    product-level prose, not a pivot, not filterable, not per-variation
'  - the "no image anywhere" case, and the real fallback chain that
'    resolves it (App\Support\ResolveVariationImage), not a null

title Product variability — two real shapes, as instances (not classes)

' ============================================================
'  PRODUCT 1 — Classic Tee: variations, shared images, specs
' ============================================================

package "Classic Tee — variations, images, specifications" {

  object "product : Products" as product {
    id = 1
    name = "Classic Tee"
    regular_price = 20.00 <b>(the fallback)</b>
  }

  object "red-front.jpg : ProductImage" as img10 {
    id = 10
  }
  object "red-back.jpg : ProductImage" as img11 {
    id = 11
  }
  object "red-detail.jpg : ProductImage" as img14 {
    id = 14
  }
  object "blue-front.jpg : ProductImage" as img12 {
    id = 12
  }
  object "blue-back.jpg : ProductImage" as img15 {
    id = 15
  }
  object "size-chart.jpg : ProductImage" as img13 {
    id = 13
    <b>belongs to no variation, ever</b>
  }

  object "TEE-RED-S : ProductVariation" as v100 {
    id = 100
    sku = "TEE-RED-S"
    price_override = <color:blue>null</color> <b>(inherits 20.00)</b>
  }
  object "TEE-RED-M : ProductVariation" as v101 {
    id = 101
    sku = "TEE-RED-M"
    price_override = 22.00 <b>(own price)</b>
  }
  object "TEE-BLUE-S : ProductVariation" as v102 {
    id = 102
    sku = "TEE-BLUE-S"
  }
  object "TEE-BLUE-M : ProductVariation" as v103 {
    id = 103
    sku = "TEE-BLUE-M"
  }

  object "Red : AttributeValue" as valRed
  object "Blue : AttributeValue" as valBlue
  object "S : AttributeValue" as valS
  object "M : AttributeValue" as valM

  object "spec: Material : ProductSpecification" as spec1 {
    name = "Material"
    value = "100% Cotton"
  }
  object "spec: Care : ProductSpecification" as spec2 {
    name = "Care"
    value = "Machine wash cold"
  }

  product "1" *-- "4" v100 : has variations
  product "1" *-- "5" img10 : owns the ONLY\ncopies of these files
  img11 : (same product)
  img12 : (same product)
  img13 : (same product)
  img14 : (same product)
  img15 : (same product)
  product "1" *-- "2" spec1 : has specifications
  spec2 : (same product)

  ' --- Pivot 1: identity. "what IS this variation" ---
  v100 -[#green]-> valRed : attribute_value_product_variation\n(IDENTITY)
  v100 -[#green]-> valS
  v101 -[#green]-> valRed
  v101 -[#green]-> valM
  v102 -[#green]-> valBlue
  v102 -[#green]-> valS
  v103 -[#green]-> valBlue
  v103 -[#green]-> valM

  ' --- Pivot 2: appearance. "what does it LOOK LIKE" ---
  ' v100: THREE images on one variation (multi-image).
  v100 -[#blue]-> img10 : product_image_product_variation\n(APPEARANCE) pos=1
  v100 -[#blue]-> img11 : pos=2
  v100 -[#blue]-> img14 : pos=3 (3rd image, same variation)

  ' v101: reuses img10/img11 exactly — same gallery as v100 (many
  ' variations to the same images, no overlap ambiguity).
  v101 -[#blue]-> img10 : pos=1 <b>(reused file)</b>
  v101 -[#blue]-> img11 : pos=2

  ' v102 and v103: PARTIAL overlap. Both show img12, but v103 ALSO
  ' shows img15, which v102 does not. Non-empty union, neither a
  ' subset of the other, neither identical.
  v102 -[#blue]-> img12 : pos=1
  v103 -[#blue]-> img12 : pos=1 <b>(shared with v102)</b>
  v103 -[#blue]-> img15 : pos=2 (v103 only — v102 does NOT show this)
}

note bottom of valRed
  <b>The one distinction worth holding onto:</b>
  Red and S are BOTH AttributeValue rows —
  one vocabulary table, used by two DIFFERENT
  pivots for two DIFFERENT questions. A class
  diagram would show one association and hide
  this; the green vs. blue edges here are the
  same table used twice, on purpose.
end note

note right of v100
  <b>Computed, not stored</b> — this is
  the other thing a class diagram
  cannot show. price_override=null
  means ResolveVariationPrice::current()
  returns the PRODUCT's regular_price
  (20.00) at read time. Nothing here is
  copied or kept in sync; there is no
  second value to go stale.
end note

note right of spec1
  <b>A third, unrelated table.</b> Free-text
  name/value on the PRODUCT, not a pivot,
  not per-variation, not filterable —
  "Cotton", "cotton" and "100% Cotton" are
  three ungroupable values no query can
  filter on. The right home for prose a
  customer reads but nobody searches by.
  See explanation/product-variability.md,
  "Why not product_specifications for the
  descriptive half."
end note

note right of img13
  Product-level-only image.
  Normal, not orphaned — a
  variation never has to
  claim every product image.
end note

' ============================================================
'  PRODUCT 2 — Plain Mug: the simplest legal shape, no images
' ============================================================

package "Plain Mug — no variations beyond one, NO images anywhere" {

  object "product : Products" as product2 {
    id = 2
    name = "Plain Mug"
    regular_price = 8.00
  }

  object "MUG-PLAIN : ProductVariation" as v200 {
    id = 200
    sku = "MUG-PLAIN"
    price_override = <color:blue>null</color>
  }

  product2 "1" *-- "1" v200 : has variations\n(single SKU — a product\nneeds no more than one)

  note right of product2
    <b>Zero rows in product_images for this product_id.</b>
    Legal — write-rules/product.md records it as such. No
    AttributeValue pivots either: a single-variation product
    needs no differentiating axis.
  end note

  note bottom of v200
    <b>ResolveVariationImage::urlOrDefault()</b>
    walks the real fallback chain and hits
    every rung:
    1. own gallery — empty, skip
    2. product's is_main image — none exists, skip
    3. images/default-product.png — the
       checked-in placeholder, shipped with
       the app, an admin never uploads it
    Never returns null to a caller that
    wants something to render.
  end note
}

@enduml
